# CSS Scroll-Driven Animations — Animating Based on Scroll Position

## 1. What Are Scroll-Driven Animations?

Normally, CSS animations run based on time. For example, an element might fade in over two seconds.

But what if an animation should progress according to how far a user scrolls?

That's where **CSS Scroll-Driven Animations** come in.

They allow developers to connect an animation's progress to a scrolling position instead of relying only on time.

For example:
- A progress bar grows as you scroll down a page.
- An image gradually fades into view.
- A heading changes size as the page scrolls.
- A reading progress indicator follows the user's position.

## 2. Why Are They Useful?

Scroll-driven animations can help create:

- Reading progress bars.
- Scroll-based storytelling.
- Image reveal effects.
- Interactive landing pages.
- Timeline animations.
- Scroll-linked visual effects.

A major advantage is that many of these effects can be created directly in CSS without manually tracking scroll events in JavaScript.

## 3. Understanding Scroll Progress Timelines

A scroll progress timeline tracks the progress of a scroll container between its scroll boundaries.

CSS provides the `scroll()` function to define this timeline.

```css
.progress {
    animation: grow linear;
    animation-timeline: scroll();
}

@keyframes grow {
    from {
        width: 0%;
    }

    to {
        width: 100%;
    }
}
```

As the user scrolls through the page, the animation progresses.

The timeline is connected to scrolling rather than elapsed time.

## 4. Building a Reading Progress Bar

Let's create a simple progress bar that fills as the user scrolls.

### HTML

```html
<div class="progress"></div>

<main>
    <h1>Understanding CSS</h1>
    <p>Scroll through this page to see the progress bar.</p>
</main>
```

### CSS

```css
.progress {
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    height: 5px;
    background: blue;
    width: 100%;
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: left;
    animation: progress-fill linear;
    animation-timeline: scroll(root block);
}

@keyframes progress-fill {
    to {
        transform: scaleX(1);
    }
}

main {
    min-height: 200vh;
    padding: 30px;
}
```

### How does it work?

- `position: fixed` keeps the progress bar at the top.
- `scaleX(0)` starts with no visible width.
- `scroll(root block)` tracks vertical scrolling of the root scroll container.
- `animation-timeline` connects the animation to that scrolling progress.
- `scaleX(1)` represents the fully expanded bar.

The bar gradually fills as the user scrolls down.

## 5. Understanding View Progress Timelines

There are two important types of scroll-driven timelines.

| Timeline | What it tracks |
|---|---|
| Scroll Progress Timeline | Progress through a scroll container's scrollable area |
| View Progress Timeline | Progress of an element as it moves through its scrollport |

A view progress timeline is especially useful when you want an animation to follow an individual element as it enters, passes through, and leaves the visible scrolling area.

## 6. Using `view()` for Scroll-Based Effects

The `view()` function creates a view progress timeline.

Example:

```html
<div class="card">
    <h2>Scroll to Reveal</h2>
    <p>This card animates as it moves through the viewport.</p>
</div>
```

```css
.card {
    animation: reveal linear both;
    animation-timeline: view();
}

@keyframes reveal {
    from {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateY(80px);
    }

    to {
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateY(0);
    }
}
```

As the card moves through the scrollport, its animation progresses according to its view timeline.

## 7. Understanding Animation Range

You can control which portion of an element's view progress timeline drives its animation using `animation-range`.

```css
.card {
    animation: reveal linear both;
    animation-timeline: view();
    animation-range: entry 0% cover 40%;
}
```

Some useful range names include:

| Range | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `entry` | When the element enters the scrollport |
| `contain` | When the element is contained within the scrollport |
| `cover` | The overall passage through the scrollport |
| `exit` | When the element leaves the scrollport |

These ranges help control when an animation begins and ends.

## 8. Scroll-Driven Animations vs Traditional Animations

| Feature | Traditional CSS Animations | Scroll-Driven Animations |
|---|---|---|
| Progress depends on | Time | Scroll position |
| Main property | `animation-duration` | `animation-timeline` |
| Common use | Loading animations and transitions | Reading progress and scroll effects |
| JavaScript required? | No | No, for supported CSS effects |

They can also be combined with other CSS animation features.

## 9. Common Mistakes

- Assuming `animation-timeline` is supported in every browser.
- Forgetting to define the keyframes.
- Confusing `scroll()` with `view()`.
- Assuming a scroll-driven animation runs according to seconds.
- Forgetting that the scroll container must have a meaningful scroll range.
- Ignoring reduced-motion preferences when designing animated experiences.

## 10. Accessibility Considerations

Animations can be distracting or uncomfortable for some users.

You can respect the user's reduced-motion preference:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .card {
        animation: none;
    }
}
```

Always make sure important information remains accessible even when animations are disabled.

## 11. Key Takeaways

- Scroll-driven animations connect CSS animations to scrolling.
- `scroll()` creates a scroll progress timeline.
- `view()` creates a view progress timeline.
- `animation-timeline` connects an animation to a timeline.
- `animation-range` controls the portion of the timeline used.
- These animations can often be implemented without JavaScript.
- Browser compatibility should be checked before using them in production.

## One-Line Summary

**CSS Scroll-Driven Animations allow developers to synchronize visual effects with scrolling progress instead of relying only on time-based animation.**
